|\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (-2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-2 \\\\\nΔz &= z^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x$, $y$, and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$, $\\Delta y$ to $y$, and $\\Delta z$ to $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + (-2) =-1 \\\\\nz_{new} &= z + Δz =-2 + 3 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx -1\n$$" 13194139535839 false] [17592186221114 1145 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-3y & =2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-4x{y}^{′}+3y+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = -4xz + 3y + 2x.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0) = 