e problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{8}{3}.$" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369431 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369432 13194139535839 true] [17592186369431 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369433 13194139535839 true] [17592186369431 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535839 true] [17592186369431 1041 "field-1" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369431 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369432 13194139535839 true] [17592186369431 1110 #uuid "af3628eb-8cd7-57b7-b508-e48099a0aec9" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369432 1034 "2" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369432 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535839 true] [17592186369432 1111 #uuid "f7aea145-0879-5d1f-b0fa-806fcc819203" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369433 1034 "1" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369433 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535839 true] [17592186369433 1111 #uuid "39e1229a-6c14-5ad0-b316-af2a299e3cca" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369434 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369435 13194139535839 true] [17592186369434 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535839 true] [17592186369434 1041 "field-2" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369434 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369435 13194139535839 true] [17592186369434 1110 #uuid "8adc27d1-fb26-5129-b368-6b1d7dd4e01c" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369435 1034 "\\frac{8}{3}" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535839 true] [17592186369435 1111 #uuid "56d146bd-c7f4-5a8b-b705-fe794b58ff55" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369436 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369437 13194139535839 true] [17592186369436 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369441 13194139535839 true] [17592186369436 1089 "q-333875" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369436 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535839 true] [17592186369436 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2