 y{y}^{″} & =x-3{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x-3{y}^{′}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(-1)={y}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(-1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y},\\, & z(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=1$ and $z(-1)=-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{-1-3(-2)}{1}=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot 1=-2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot 1=5 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{0-3\\cdot 3}{(-1)}=9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+9=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $9$ | $9$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(1)=z(1)≈12.$" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369465 13194139535839 true] [17592186369464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369466 13194139535839 true] [17592186369464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369467 13194139535839 true] [17592186369464 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535839 true] [17592186369464 1041 "field-1" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369464 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369466 13194139535839 true] [17592186369464 1110 #uuid "822056ab-1455-5747-aa00-c3cf79a2db2e" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369465 1034 "3" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535839 true] [17592186369465 1111 #uuid "b7308a9e-70c1-5a26-a72a-cfddc38b83d6" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369466 1034 "2" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535839 true] [17592186369466 1111 #uuid "941ba567-b5b4-5d36-99ff-2397de8e235f" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369467 1034 "1" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535839 true] [17592186369467 1111 #uuid "a2f84225-f4e4-5b76-a7f4-8df05cff74fe" 13194139535839 true] [17592186369468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369469 13194139535839 true] [17592186369468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369470 13194139535839 true] [17592186369468 1000 #e